  <li><b>[DROPPED 10-16 by Glenn: 426M tfmr vs v3 &mdash; v3 superseded; 150M matched v4-architecture test first, then 1B] 10-15 16:40 (Glenn) SPEED UP TRANSFORMER BASELINE TRAINING</b> for the v3 comparison (426M <code>temp/tfmr426_L17.yaml</code>, 10B tok, on the 5090 after the demo; est. 3&ndash;5 d). Research + CPU / idle-GPU experiments allowed. status: researched 10-15 17:00, bench to-test (needs a free 5090 hour). FINDINGS, ranked: <b>(1) no quality change</b>: (a) bigger micro-batch on the 5090 (32 GB; the 5080 probe was memory-capped at B4); (b) pick the SDPA backend: FlashAttention-3/4 do not run on sm_120 (no TMEM), FA2 sm_120 support is partial; cuDNN SDPA is reported as the sm_120 path &mdash; time flash vs cuDNN vs efficient; (c) the transformer runs inside the spiking LM shell: <code>HybridAttentionBlock.forward_sequence</code> allocates a zero (B,T,1280) spike tensor per layer for telemetry, plus <code>use_fpt</code>/state pass-through &mdash; profile and skip when all layers are attention; (d) fewer evals; CUDA graphs only if GA1. <b>(2) small numerics change, needs a 30M parity check</b>: FP8 matmuls (torchao Float8Linear; modded-nanogpt uses FP8 head + MLP). GeForce Blackwell FP8 peak = 2&times; bf16, but no published 5090 training benchmark and naive FP8 can be slower &mdash; must confirm FP8 kernels run. Guess 1.2&ndash;1.5&times;. <b>(3) recipe changes = change the comparison (Glenn decides)</b>: drop MTP (v3 has it; ~10&ndash;15%); Muon at the transformer-tuned LR (0.02 at 363M) = better per token, not faster. <b>Glenn 10-15 17:05: keep the comparison EXACT</b> (same MTP heads, data, tokens, schedule as v3; no recipe changes a reviewer could call unfair) &rarr; group (3) dropped; FP8 skipped too (Glenn 17:10: keep bf16 like v3); only speedups that leave the math unchanged. <b>17:30 CODE REVIEW (Glenn: fork train_gpu_tran.py only if big gains)</b>: VERDICT no fork. Spiking-only trainer code is config-gated off; the LM shell&rsquo;s per-layer zero states / spike stubs are unused outputs that whole-model compile drops; MTP / z-loss / MQAR are part of the v3 recipe and stay. The gains are existing config keys that keep the math: (i) same tokens per step, bigger micro-batch: B36&times;GA2 or B72&times;GA1 instead of B18&times;GA4 (MQAR samples per step stay 72); (ii) <code>mqar_once_per_step</code> = one MQAR batch of 72 instead of 4 of 18 (same samples and weight, fewer small launch-bound passes; cost was ~32% of the step on the 1B spiking run); (iii) <code>mqar_pad_to: 65</code> + <code>mqar_compile_static</code> = one static MQAR graph instead of dynamic shapes (causal, so pads cannot change the answer); (iv) SDPA backend (flash / cuDNN / efficient). Guess 10&ndash;25% total, unmeasured. Config: move <code>temp/tfmr426_L17.yaml</code> to <code>configs/</code> with these keys after the bench. BENCH PLAN (~1 h, 5090 after the demo): 200 steps each of B4/8/12/16 &times; SDPA backend (bf16); log tok/s, peak VRAM, loss match.</li>
