  <li><b>10-14 11:00 (Neuro 61) CHECKPOINT AVERAGING &mdash; LITERATURE (Glenn asked: how many checkpoints?)</b>: LAWA (Kaddour 2022) k=6 default, k&gt;16 hurts, insensitive in between; LAWA for LLMs (Sanyal 2023) k=5, gains biggest at high LR / mid-run, shrinking to &ldquo;minor&rdquo; at the end of the decay; PMA (ByteDance Seed 2025) N=3/6/10/15 tested, after full training N=15 best and N=3 ~1 point lower, but more models HURT early (unstable weights); used N=6 for 411M / 2B dense, N=10 for 8B / 70B, interval ~2&ndash;8B tokens; WMA &gt; SMA &gt; EMA early; WSM (ICLR 2026) the WINDOW (merge duration) matters most, then interval, then count (fixed 80B window: 8 ckpts 63.78, 16 63.63, 4 63.36, 2 62.77, 1 60.33), and merging AFTER a full LR decay adds nothing; Llama 3 averages checkpoints during the final anneal (count not given); DeepSeek-V3 keeps a per-step EMA of the weights on CPU to preview post-decay quality (no checkpoint averaging, no decay value given). Our checkpoints are ~6&ndash;7.5k steps (~0.2&ndash;0.25B tok) apart, far below PMA's intervals. Test (status: queued) <code>temp/queue_5080_n61avgk.sh</code> &rarr; <code>logs/n61_avgk.log</code>: k=1..7 + s3 (same count, 2&times; window) + w5 (WMA 5:4:3:2:1), each with ppl, cross-chunk recall, spikes, dead units, energy-v2, HellaSwag / ARC-E. Caveat: the final conversion checkpoints are at low LR, where the literature expects a smaller gain &rarr; repeat on the last conversion checkpoints (to-test).</li>
