  <li><b style="color:#c0392b">10-13 18:00 (Neuro 60) DATA CHECK (Glenn 17:05): the "alive share" was measured wrong a SECOND time; corrected picture; research; fix list. SUPERSEDES the 17:45 entry below.</b> <b>1. What the tools really count</b> (independent re-measurement with my own hooks, <code>temp/n60_verify.py</code> &rarr; <code>logs/n60_verify.log</code>, <code>logs/n60_verify_159k.log</code>; both tools reproduce to 3 digits, so neither has a code bug): the trainer&rsquo;s <code>[mlp]</code> rate and the homeostat count &ldquo;pre-activation &gt; threshold&rdquo;; <code>temp/n59_health.py</code> counts &ldquo;output not exactly 0&rdquo;. An ERASED unit (input row decayed to ~1e-20; many rows are now exactly 0) has a pre-activation of ~1e-25 that is &ldquo;positive&rdquo; on about half the tokens: the trainer counts it as firing (blocks 1&ndash;5 print 0.33&ndash;0.45, real 0.02&ndash;0.08), and the scorecard counted it as alive until its squared output fell under what bf16 can hold. <b>Functional test = pre-activation above 1e-2 at least once on 65k val tokens</b>: 47.2% (46,500), 49.3% (90,000), 50.4% (142,500), 50.9% (159,000) = FLAT. The old numbers on the same checkpoints: 50.8 / 72.8 / 63.5 / 59.8%. So &ldquo;77% alive&rdquo; (Neuro 59) and &ldquo;falling to 60%&rdquo; (me, 17:45) were both artefacts; nothing is collapsing. Per block at 159,000 (functional): 0:78% 1:30% 2:0% 3:2% 4:0% 5:0% 7:100% 8:67% 9:66% 10:80% 11:89% 12:100%; block 0 is recovering (26% at 46,500), block 2 lost its last 13%. Exactly-zero input rows at 159,000: blk1 25%, blk2 58%, blk3 47%, blk4 63%, blk5 44% (142,500: 0&ndash;3%): the erased rows are now underflowing to 0. <b>2. What the model uses</b>: <code>temp/n60_blockoff.py</code> (<code>logs/n60_blockoff.log</code>, 159,000, ppl 31.658): FFNs of blocks 0&ndash;5 ALL off = +0.67% (block 1 alone +0.55%, blocks 2&ndash;5 exactly 0); one upper FFN off = +2.9% (blk7), +3.6, +4.5, +5.5, +11.0, +27.4% (blk12). <code>temp/n60_prune.py</code> (<code>logs/n60_prune.log</code>): removing every unit whose pre-activation never exceeds 1e-2 = 49.1% of the FFN units &rarr; 813M parameters, ppl +0.01%; never above 0.1 = 56.3% &rarr; 769M, ppl &minus;0.04%. The model is 12 attention layers + the memory + SIX working FFNs; 27&ndash;31% of the parameters are idle and have been since before step 46,500. Dead half = the blocks BEFORE the memory layer. <b>3. Other checks</b>: threshold ramp &rarr; block 1&rsquo;s threshold (0.0047) only removed erased flicker, its real units are unchanged (30.3% before and after); in blocks 2 / 3 / 5 the homeostat now flips between 0 and ~0.004 every step (harmless, nothing real there), so the <code>[mlp]</code> line is NOT a health signal. Schedule arithmetic (70% = step 159,363; target 0.374 at 159k; tok/s 37.1k &times; 32,768 / 40,960 = 29.7k with the 16 &times; 512 haystack batches) checks out. Val eval = fixed windows, 410k tokens: the fall 31.8 &rarr; 27.8 is real. Memory spike rate: trainer 0.004 vs diagnostic 0.0032 at 159k = consistent. LR: cosine with a 10% floor, now 24% of peak; conversion restarts at ~27%. Corrections to my own messages: the &ldquo;alive at 90,000 (control)&rdquo; row was the home probe at 90,300; &ldquo;embedding decay caused it&rdquo; and &ldquo;the printed rates come from recall batches&rdquo; were wrong guesses. Still broken, to-fix: <code>temp/n58_layer_diag.py</code> prints stream RMS = nan; <code>temp/n58_revive.py</code> / the health check&rsquo;s &ldquo;dead&rdquo; line use &ldquo;never &gt; 0&rdquo; (misses erased units). Cross-chunk recall on the cloud checkpoints is at chance through 159,000, as designed (its training starts at 159,363): the first real reading is 166,500 (RUNNING <code>temp/n60_early166.sh</code> &rarr; <code>logs/n60_early166.log</code>). <b>4. Research (10-13)</b>: <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27715">Aurora 2606.27715</a> (Muon starves rows of tall matrices; with ReLU&sup2; the low-leverage neurons collapse early and never recover = our pattern; gain grows with FFN expansion, ours is 6&times;; no mid-run switch tested; 1.1B table: Aurora 57.2, Muon 56.0, U-NorMuon 55.5, NorMuon 55.1); <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12902">ReDo 2302.12902</a> (new input weights + zero output weights = what our revival does); <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.04381">DSD 1607.04381</a> (pruned weights restored FROM ZERO and retrained: +1.1 to +4.3 points on ImageNet = evidence that re-densing dead capacity late pays); <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.06211">Staged training 2203.06211</a> (growth must preserve the loss AND the training dynamics: optimizer state + schedule for the new weights; up to 22% compute saved); <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.05125">GradMax 2201.05125</a> (new input rows from the SVD of the gradient, so new units learn fastest); <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05641">Net2Net 1511.05641</a> (split a live unit in two, function-preserving); <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.24061">2505.24061</a> (dormant units do not recover alone; judge by gradient, not activation); literature warnings: reborn units that only add noise get ignored and die again, and a reset is a shock that should be scaled in; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.03241">LAWA 2306.03241</a> (averaging the latest checkpoints: val loss 2.977 &rarr; 2.908 at 770M, beats EMA / SWA; tested on Pythia 1&ndash;12B); <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23198">Sakana 2603.23198</a> (1&ndash;2B ReLU models, &gt;99% activation sparsity within 2% loss; dead-neuron mitigation named as their open lever). <b>5. Fix list (status)</b>: (F1) <b>functional scorecard</b> = built (<code>n60_verify.py</code>, <code>n60_prune.py</code>, <code>n60_blockoff.py</code>); use these, not the alive line of n59_health. (F2) <b>revival, tested where the cloud is now</b> = RUNNING: <code>temp/queue_5090_n60r166.sh</code> then <code>temp/queue_5090_n60u166.sh</code> &rarr; <code>logs/n60_r166.log</code>, all from step 166,500 to 167,500 with <code>temp/n60_revive.py</code> (dead = pre-activation never above 1e-3): C166 control, R166 all dead units, U166 blocks 7&ndash;12 only (~12k units, where FFNs matter), L166 blocks 1&ndash;5 only (~60k units). If clean, apply at the START OF CONVERSION (restore point = the dense-final checkpoint; Llama teacher signal; LR back at 27%; 93k steps), Glenn&rsquo;s OK needed. (F3) to-build if F2 is promising: gentler revival = waves, update scale of revived rows ramped in, input rows from split live units (Net2Net) or the gradient SVD (GradMax); per-unit threshold (<code>attn_ffn_homeo: unit</code>) so the layer threshold does not silence the newly revived units first. (F4) <b>hygiene pass at the dense &rarr; conversion seed</b>, to-build (small, exact): set erased rows / columns to exactly 0 so the homeostat, the <code>[mlp]</code> line and the spike / energy counts see only real firing. (F5) <b>prune and say so</b> = fallback with zero risk, to-decide with Glenn: ship 0.77&ndash;0.81B with identical perplexity, 100% of the shipped parameters in use, less energy and memory. (F6) <b>checkpoint averaging (LAWA)</b>, to-test at home, zero cloud risk: average the last pulled checkpoints, fixed-seed ppl vs the newest alone; if it helps, use it for the final demo weights. (F7) memory: judge the falling &beta; (0.45 &rarr; 0.25, 4% at floor) by cross-chunk recall at 166,500 / 174,000; lever if recall does not form = cross-chunk share 0.25 &rarr; 0.5 (config, needs OK). (F8) parked / closed: ghost in any form (W1 stopped at step 90,290: val 30.95 at 90,200 vs control 32.55, full ghost 31.46; W2 and S90 not run), per-unit boost W3 (revival is cleaner). (F9) v5: Aurora or U-NorMuon on the FFN input matrices, no decay on zero-gradient rows from step 0, soft activation first / hard zero later (ProSparse), 4&times; FFN, functional-alive telemetry + tripwire from step 0, find why only the blocks before the memory die.</li>
  <li><b>10-13 15:30 (Neuro 60) FAIR TRANSFORMER COMPARISON at 1B &mdash; options (Glenn asked; status: to-test, none built)</b>. (a) <b>free</b>: memory-off ablation of v4 itself + the matched 13L controls already run (r26o 36.55 vs attention-only 38.84). (b) <b>layer swap</b> (parked until Glenn picks): from the finished DENSE v4, replace the one spiking memory block with a standard attention block, fine-tune both versions on the same extra tokens on the 5090, compare perplexity / recall / energy; base-to-base, no post-training needed. (c) <b>outside reference</b>: Llama-3.2-1B on our val text in bits per byte (tokenizer-free; not token-matched, it saw far more data). (d) <b>from-scratch matched 1.1B transformer</b>: the 5090 fits 1B only at B1&times;GA2 = weeks (benchmark first); cloud ~2 days but no budget. Comparing the POST-TRAINED models needs the same conversion-phase distillation + SFT on the transformer. Also 10-13: the cloud tok/s step-down 37.1k &rarr; ~32k at step 159,363 = <code>mqar_gap_start_frac 0.7</code> (haystack recall batches, 512 tokens), scheduled, not a fault; theta is a persistent buffer, a resume is safe.</li>
  <li><b>10-12 23:55 (Neuro 59) IDEA (Glenn): MIXTURE OF BRAINS ("MoB")</b>, status parked / to-design, for the models after the 1B: a large model names the expertise it needs &rarr; a small model with many brains routes the query to the right brain(s) &rarr; the answers go back to the large model. <b>My notes</b>: a brain is a small weight add-on (LoRA) trained ON one base model, so it only works with that model: "one small model + many swappable brains" is the natural form (one copy of the small model in memory, brains are a few MB each); the large model sees it as a tool ("ask the expert on X"). Close published relatives: LoraHub, X-LoRA, mixture-of-LoRA-experts, agent delegation. Gains: new knowledge without retraining the large model, brains train in ~30 min, private / local, the small model does the expert work (energy). Risks: the small model caps the answer quality; routing mistakes; text hand-off loses detail; the large model must treat a brain's answer as evidence (needs the brain to say "not in my brain"). <b>First cheap test, to-build at 13L</b>: brain router with the three existing brains (Python, health, mechanics): pick the brain from the query, load it, answer; compare with always-the-right-brain and with all brains merged at once (interference). <b>Cloud status 23:54</b>: step 102,600 of 227,661 (45%), 37.0k tok/s; val 36.74 (93k), <b>35.48</b> (96k, best so far, still decay off), 39.72 (99k), 36.91 (102k, first eval after the rollback at 99,802); embed norm 876 &rarr; 823 after the rollback; [mlp] block 4 still 0.000, block 0 0.13, blocks 1&ndash;3 0.27&ndash;0.36, block 5 0.21.</li>
  <li><b>10-12 23:45 (Neuro 59) 5080 tonight + BRAIN IDEAS (Glenn)</b>. <b>RESULT 01:28</b>: Python brain with 1:1 replay = quiz still 100%, brain ON mode .955, tool .992, args .869, refuse .885 (base .970 / .984 / .820 / .923; old brain .925 / .984 / .795 / .808) = most of the gap closed, refusal still 3.8 points short; follow-up running <code>temp/queue_5080_n59brainrep2.sh</code> (health brain 1:1, Python brain 2:1; <code>logs/n59_brainrep2.log</code>; new env settings FDCBRAIN_OUT / FDCBRAIN_REPLAY). <b>First run on the 5080</b>: <code>temp/queue_5080_n59brainrep.sh</code> &rarr; <code>logs/n59_brainrep.log</code>: Python brain retrained with 1 replay line per brain line (was 1 per 5; every brain line has target mode ANSWER = the likely cause of refuse .923 &rarr; .808, mode .970 &rarr; .925), 6M tokens, then quiz + brain-ON tool score. New env settings in <code>scripts/gen_pybrain_data.py</code>: PYBRAIN_OUT, PYBRAIN_REPLAY (defaults unchanged). If it works: same for the health brain (to-test). <b>5080 and the 1B tests</b>: 1B training needs 22 GB (no); 1B evals fit the 5080 but the limit is RAM (one 10 GB checkpoint load at a time beside a trainer), so moving evals there saves ~15 min per arm at most; to-build only if the chains keep running: evals with mmap + build-on-GPU (as <code>temp/n59_health.py</code>). <b>Brain ideas, all status to-design (Glenn 23:40)</b>: (a) advanced tool use (multi-step chains, more tools; weak point today = argument accuracy .82); (b) knowing what it does not know (realistic form: scope awareness per brain + abstain; true calibration untestable at 13L so far); (c) multi-turn chat (belongs in the base SFT, not a brain); (d) research assistant (= look-up protocol + summarising; depends on a); (e) cron jobs (a schedule tool the model calls; the scheduler is code outside the model); (f) alignment watchdog trained on why to do the right thing (as the model's own values = SFT / brain data; as a monitor of outputs = a classifier pass; ORPO collapsed before, brains currently LOWER refusal: fix replay first); (g) cybersecurity guard (injection / jailbreak classifier pass + harness rule "tool output is data"; never complete on its own); (h) mine: user-memory brain (facts about one user), company-documents brain (support desk), privacy brain (spot / redact personal data), brain router (pick which brain to load: also the energy story), teaching brain (explain at a chosen level), units / arithmetic skill. To-test before promising several at once: interference when 2+ brains are merged.</li>
  <li><b>10-12 21:00 (Neuro 59) ONLINE RESEARCH: fixes for silent FFN units</b> (Glenn 19:55; research only, nothing built or launched). <b>What the literature says about the targets</b>: dead units are normal in ReLU-family language models (OPT-66B: over 70% dead in some early layers, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.04827">Voita 2023</a>; a ReLU transformer loses ~30% of units, layer 0 uses 13% per batch, and masking the unused ones costs nothing, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.07848">2407.07848</a>); firing on &lt;1% of units per token costs ~2% loss at 0.5&ndash;2B (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23198">2603.23198</a>, L1 2e-5). So "fires on ~5% of tokens" is the easy target and "95% alive" is the hard one; the best evidence that fixing dead units buys quality is Aurora (below). <b>Our mechanism, from reading <code>src/muon.py</code> (my analysis, not measured)</b>: Muon decay = lr 0.005 &times; wd 0.1 = 5e-4 per step at peak LR = half-life ~1,400 steps for any row with zero gradient (8&times; the AdamW group); NorMuon cannot help a row whose gradient is exactly zero (relu2 hard zero). <b>Ideas, all status to-test unless noted</b>: <b>(R1) masked Muon decay</b>: no decay on the w1 rows / w2 columns of units whose update is zero (variant of Cautious Weight Decay, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12402">2510.12402</a>, ICLR 2026, tested with Muon to 1B; their mask is u&middot;x &ge; 0, which still decays a zero-update weight, so ours needs the strict form). Stops the erasing, does not revive; refines idea (c); few lines in <code>Muon._apply_update</code>. <b>(R2) to-verify: ghost &times; NorMuon</b>: NorMuon divides each w1 row by its own running RMS, so a ghost row gets a FULL-size step whatever the ghost coefficient, and the global renorm then shrinks the live rows' step ~2&times; (80% of rows join). Prediction: arm G behaves like the harmful broad revival, not like a gentle nudge; if so, fix = keep ghost rows out of the row normalisation or scale them by the coefficient. <b>(R3) U-NorMuon / Aurora</b> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27715">2606.27715</a>, Tilde, code github.com/tilde-research/aurora-release): Muon starves low-leverage rows of TALL matrices (= our FFN w1, 12288&times;2048) in a self-reinforcing loop; &gt;25% of neurons dead by step 500; 1.1B: loss 2.26 Aurora vs 2.31 Muon vs 2.33 NorMuon; gain grows with FFN expansion (ours 6&times;). U-NorMuon = stateless row normalisation on tall matrices only (free); Aurora K=2 costs ~24% speed on one GPU. From-scratch evidence only, SwiGLU (no hard zero): for v5, or with R4. <b>(R4) leak in training, exact zero at the end</b> = idea (b) with a template: ProSparse (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.13516">2402.13516</a>) trains with a soft activation, then a slowly rising L1, then shifts the threshold: 89% sparsity at 7B, 87.9% at 1B, quality kept. <b>(R5) per-unit homeostat with boost</b>: <code>attn_ffn_homeo: unit</code> exists, but theta is clamped at 0 so it can only silence; a per-unit bias that also goes negative (duty-cycle boosting, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.11257">Numenta 1903.11257</a>; intrinsic plasticity) pushes every unit toward the 5% rate = the target itself. Needs R6 for erased units. <b>RISK to-check</b>: the cloud's layer-wide threshold ramp starts at 60% (step ~136.6k, ~08:30 on 10-13 by my estimate); one threshold per layer silences the weak units first and decay then erases them: watch the alive share across the ramp. <b>(R6) recycle at the start of conversion / SFT</b> (ReDo <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12902">2302.12902</a>; Anthropic resampling): dead unit = new input row (toward inputs on high-loss tokens, ~0.2&times; the mean live norm), output column ZERO (loss unchanged at step 0), optimizer state of those rows reset. <code>temp/n58_revive.py</code> already zeroes the output column, and broad revival was still harmful (72.31); what the literature does differently = small input norm, data-driven rows, optimizer reset, and a small share of units per period (continual backprop, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.13812">2306.13812</a>) instead of all at once. Later papers: gains mixed; pick by gradient, not activation (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.24061">2505.24061</a>). <b>(R7) AuxK for the conversion</b> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.04093">OpenAI 2406.04093</a>: removes almost all dead latents, ~10% cost): the top-k dead units get an extra loss to predict what the FFN still misses against the teacher. My adaptation to a language model, unproven. <b>(R8) transcoder route, parked</b> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11944">2406.11944</a>): replace each FFN after training by a wide TopK layer trained layer by layer (cheap on the 5090), then a short KD pass; reaches both targets by construction, costs some ppl, biggest build, good for the interpretability story. <b>(R9) prune instead of revive</b>: removing never-firing units is exactly function-preserving: same ppl from ~0.65B parameters, "share in use" ~100% by shrinking the model; to-decide with Glenn (changes the 1.1B headline). <b>(R10) v5 note</b>: 6&times; FFN under Muon is where neuron death is worst; either R3 or a narrower FFN. Closed by the literature: plain shrink-and-perturb / L2 toward init (continual-learning tools, no LM-scale evidence).</li>
  <li><b style="color:#c0392b">10-12 01:15 (Neuro 57) DEMO PLAN + DESIGNS WRITTEN: <code>docs/demo_v4_plan_2026_10_12.md</code> &mdash; waiting for Glenn&rsquo;s review, nothing built</b>. Glenn&rsquo;s answers (10-12): local demo on the 5090, rehearsed scenarios + free typing; internet allowed, offline demo wanted too; the model says &ldquo;I will look that up / calculate that for you&rdquo;; brains = read documents AND train weights; final SFT on the 5090; tests and training runs at home need no further OK. <b>Measured</b>: generation = 115&ndash;150 ms per token at 189M and at 1.1B alike (6&ndash;8 tok/s at 1B on the 5090; <code>logs/n57_gen_speed.log</code>), i.e. overhead: ~4,500 module calls per forward, <code>SpikeCode.forward</code> 45% (<code>logs/n57_gen_profile.log</code>); no KV cache exists for RC3 (<code>step_kv</code> refuses attn_canon and chunk crossing); training offers 2&ndash;6 tools, the demo 9. <b>Ideas, all to-build after review unless marked</b>: (D1) protocol v2 = <code>TOOL &lt;fixed sentence per tool&gt; {json}</code>, selector <code>tool_proto: 2</code>, 30% of examples with all 9 tools; (D2) executors (calculator / unit_convert / get_time local, define = WordNet, search = Wikipedia + SQLite FTS5 offline pack, weather = Open-Meteo + cache, translate = opus-mt, reminder / message simulated); (D3) second pass in the Instruction + Input format with teacher-written replies from REAL executor results (<code>toolresult.jsonl</code>, ~9k), zero-shot &ldquo;before&rdquo; number first; (D4) self-knowledge by R-Tuning: probe the SFT base checkpoint, known &rarr; ANSWER, unknown &rarr; search or &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo;, eval = selectivity with forced-answer ground truth; confidence gauge only if AUROC &ge; 0.75; (D5) multi-turn = history in a 512-token prompt, ~10k examples (half rule-built), eval with a no-history control; (D6) server on RC3: tracer fixes, energy-v2 meter, compiled fixed-length forward (target &ge; 15 tok/s), SpikeCode stats switch, offline switch, run-through script; (D7) brains = LoRA adapter (r 32 on qkv / o_proj / ffn, ~31 MB at 1B) trained on teacher-made study material (8 wordings per fact), merged into the weights at load, quiz with wrong-brain and never-taught controls + the search baseline; mechanism test at 13L with invented facts FIRST; live brain building = stretch; (D8) SFT blend + <code>sft_text_frac: 0.15</code> plain-text replay against the +49% perplexity loss (one 13L arm); (D9) <code>temp/demo_regress.sh</code>; (D10) Drive backup script. <b>Strategy</b>: SFT twice on the 5090 &mdash; SFT-A on the dense-final checkpoint (10-14, fallback model + full-scale rehearsal), SFT-B on the converted one (10-16), token budget for B chosen from A&rsquo;s curve. <b>Brain domains researched, Glenn to choose</b>: (1) own company / private documents, (2) one machine&rsquo;s manual (TechManualQA-700, Agri-Query), (3) first aid (FirstAidQA), (4) aviation (FAA handbook); recommended 1 + 2, then 3. Parked: KV cache for RC3 (days of work), brain as a memory file without weight training (plain-language cross-chunk recall ~chance), memory-layer / Engram-table brain targets (only if the LoRA mechanism test fails).</li>
  <li><b>10-11 22:50 (Neuro 56) DEMO PREP BACKLOG while v4 trains</b> (Glenn asked: does v4 know when it does not know and use tools; tools / scaffolding / other scripts for the demo). <b>State read from the code</b>: the tool data is ONE step (<code>### Tools:</code> + question &rarr; <code>TOOL {json}</code> or <code>ANSWER text</code>; tools.jsonl = 14,396 tool / 3,604 tool_absent / 6,000 answer); the model picks tool vs answer from the question type and the offered tools (v3 SFT: mode .96, tool .96, args .86 constrained), NOT from its own uncertainty (v3 SFT abstain selectivity 0; the abstain stage = ORPO collapsed, confidence head unfinished); nothing executes a tool and there is no "tool result &rarr; answer" format; 9 schemas in tools.json (search, calculator, unit_convert, get_weather, get_time, define, translate, set_reminder, send_message). Demo stage 1 exists (<code>scripts/demo/server.py</code> + index.html, 10-04: chat, spike raster, write gate, op / energy meter, memory-off replay; has a CPU reference for the exact LIF), built before RC3. The SFT format is single-turn. <b>Ideas, none built</b>: (1) tool executor + loop in the demo server (real: calculator, unit_convert, get_time, define, search, get_weather; simulated cards: set_reminder, send_message; translate = to-decide) &mdash; to-build; (2) second step by a second model pass with the result in the Alpaca <code>### Input:</code> field (in-format, no training) &mdash; to-test on the 13L rehearsal checkpoint; (3) "tool result &rarr; answer" SFT examples from the Llama-3.2-3B teacher (5080, <code>scripts/gen_teacher_data.py</code> pattern) added to the v4 SFT blend &mdash; to-build if (2) is weak; (4) "knows it does not know": turn the 9,600 unanswerable abstain pairs into plain SFT examples (search offered &rarr; <code>TOOL search</code>; not offered &rarr; an honest "I don&rsquo;t know"), plus the 6,400 answerable ones as normal answers &mdash; to-test at 13L with <code>scripts/abstain_eval.py</code>; (5) confidence head on the frozen SFT model &mdash; parked (v3 attempt degenerate); (6) port the demo server to RC3 checkpoints (chunked attention, HOLA, spike codes) and wire the energy meter to energy-v2 &mdash; to-build; (7) memory showcase: fact, &gt; 1024 tokens of filler, question, with the memory-off switch (cross-chunk haystack 100 vs 16 with memory off at 13L) &mdash; to-build, gated on the SFT rehearsal keeping the cross-chunk memory; (8) scripted demo run-through + a regression check per checkpoint (13L now, 1B later) &mdash; to-build; (9) multi-turn chat is untrained &mdash; keep the demo single-question or test history-in-prompt; (10) generation has no KV cache (full re-forward per token) &mdash; measure tok/s at 1B on the 5090 before deciding anything. Status: waiting for Glenn&rsquo;s go on the order.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Research Backlog</h2>
<p class="updated"><b>RESEARCH BACKLOG (added 2026-09-21 from Glenn's shortlists &mdash; capture so nothing is lost; NONE screened yet).</b> Legend: BUILT = code exists + unit-tested; SCREENED = quality-tested; TO-TEST = not started; DONE = shipped.</p>
<p><b>Picked for v4 post-training (Neuro 61, 2026-10-13)</b> &mdash; from the lists below; v4 SFT-A on the dense-final checkpoint (~10-14) and SFT-B on the converted one (~10-16), both on the 5090:</p>
<ol>
  <li><b>Calibrated abstention + confidence head</b> &mdash; Glenn's demo point 1 (&ldquo;I will look that up for you&rdquo;). Data and <code>head_conf</code> exist from v3, but the v3 head never trained (loss 0) and SFT abstain selectivity was 0. to-test: self-knowledge labels by probing which questions the 1B SFT answers correctly, then train answer vs search (demo backlog 10-11 22:50); the probing fits the 5080 with mmap evals.</li>
  <li><b>Tool-caller + single-shot function-call SFT</b> &mdash; already in the v4 SFT (protocol v2, <code>configs/sft_v4*</code>); nothing extra to build.</li>
  <li><b>On-policy SELF-distillation (OPSD) for brains</b> &mdash; teacher = the same model with the brain's source document in context, student = no document; trains the brain (LoRA) to answer from its weights = Glenn's &ldquo;brains read documents AND train weights&rdquo;. to-design; 13L test on the 5080 first.</li>
  <li><b>&ldquo;Sleep&rdquo; consolidation</b> &mdash; same family as OPSD (distil in-context / fast-weight state into weights); test alongside OPSD at 13L.</li>
  <li><b>On-policy distillation</b> from the v4 SFT checkpoint with the Llama-3.2-1B teacher used for conversion &mdash; only if SFT-B leaves time before 10-19 (5090). v3 lesson: start from SFT, never from ORPO (ORPO collapsed).</li>
  <li>Not for v4: &ldquo;why&rdquo; in ORPO / preference optimisation (collapsed twice at 363&ndash;500M), Distilled RL (base near chance), Dream-RSI (process idea).</li>
</ol>
<p><b>Evaluation ideas carried over from the v3 list (still open, for v4):</b></p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Benchmark inference speed vs Transformer:</b> measure <b>TTFT</b> (time-to-first-token) and <b>tok/s</b> for our model vs a size-matched Transformer baseline. <b>If we win, add the numbers to the marketing blurbs</b> (<code>H:\Projects\PsycorAI - Backup\blogs\marketing_blurbs.html</code>) &mdash; building the strongest case for investors/developers. Blurbs already cite ~34% lower ppl + ~85% inference-energy savings.</li>
  <li><b>Origin &amp; path tracing (interpretability):</b> research online how to test neuronal origin/propagation-path tracing in a spiking model (methods, metrics, prior art), dev the scripts, run it, and record results. Validates the alignment/truthfulness-auditing claim in the blurbs (currently phrased as architecture-enabled, not yet demonstrated). <b>Great blog material later.</b></li>
</ul>


<p><b>Post-training strategies (6) &mdash; screening gated on base hitting 10B (~2026-09-24/25):</b></p>
<ol>
  <li><b>On-policy distillation</b> &mdash; BUILT + GPU-smoked (<code>src/train_opd.py</code>, <code>configs/opd_v3_363m.yaml</code>). SCREEN pending base. 5090-only.</li>
  <li><b>Calibrated abstention</b> (reward "I don't know") + confidence head + act/confirm/refuse routing &mdash; BUILT (16k abstain data, <code>configs/head_conf_v3_363m.yaml</code>, <code>scripts/abstain_eval.py</code>). SCREEN pending base.</li>
  <li><b>Cactus Needle</b> &mdash; RESOLVED: = a learned confidence score on the FROZEN v3 model for harness act/confirm/refuse routing + a measurable hallucination number. This IS our confidence head (#2 above / <code>src/heads.py</code>, <code>configs/head_conf_v3_363m.yaml</code>, on-policy labels) &mdash; BUILT. Reference recipe to cross-check: <a href="https://github.com/cactus-compute/needle">github.com/cactus-compute/needle</a>. SCREEN pending base.</li>
  <li><b>Tool-caller, not knower</b> &mdash; BUILT (24k tool data + 501-node grammar DFA, <code>src/tool_data.py</code>/<code>src/grammar.py</code>, <code>scripts/tool_eval.py</code>). SCREEN pending base.</li>
  <li><b>Single-shot function-call SFT set</b> &mdash; BUILT (this is the tools SFT set from #4). SCREEN pending base.</li>
  <li><b>"why" in ORPO chosen responses</b> + energy/verifier head for best-of-N &amp; refine &mdash; BUILT (<code>configs/orpo_v3_363m.yaml</code>, <code>head_energy_v3_363m.yaml</code>); <b style="color:green">"why" data COMPLETE (6000)</b>. SCREEN pending base.</li>
  <li><b>"Sleep" consolidation stage</b> (added 2026-09-25; Behrouz et al., <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03979">arXiv 2606.03979</a>) &mdash; distil what's in the fast-weight (GDN-2/Hebbian) state into the slow weights: teacher = model WITH a doc loaded via split-replay, student = model WITHOUT it, KL on the query tokens (context distillation). Plus "dreaming": the model generates its own rehearsal data, and only samples that improve a held-out metric are kept (ReST-EM). Best-fit 2026 analogue of Glenn's Dream-RSI question. Risks: 426M base is weak (self-generated data amplifies errors); post-pretrain MQAR refine was NULL at 500M. TO-TEST, as a small screen AFTER the verified post-train run, not inserted before it.</li>
  <li><b>On-policy SELF-distillation (OPSD) with privileged info</b> (added 2026-09-25; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.18141">survey 2605.18141</a>) &mdash; the model is its own teacher, conditioned on the reference answer (for us: loaded into the fast state via split-replay). No 3B teacher, ~40&ndash;60% less memory than OPD &rarr; might fit the 5080. CAUTION <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.01763">2607.01763</a>: dense self-distillation drifts, forgets, and amplifies formatting artifacts &mdash; keep it short, mix in off-policy data. TO-TEST.</li>
  <li><b>Distilled RL</b> (added 2026-09-25; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.17247">2607.17247</a>) &mdash; teacher-guided RL that reportedly beats both GRPO and OPD. Needs verifiable rewards and a base above chance; ours is at chance on MMLU/ARC-c. PARKED until v4.</li>
</ol>
<p><b>Research-process idea (added 2026-09-25):</b> <b>Dream-RSI</b> (Google, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14858">arXiv 2609.14858</a>) trains NO weights. It improves an agent's <i>exploration policy</i> by replaying past discovery trees as free simulators. Our analogue: treat the journey/screen history (screen &rarr; outcome) as a replay tree to tune <i>our</i> screening policy (which levers to screen, when to stop a screen early). Our tree is small (~100 nodes), so value is low-to-moderate. PARKED.</p>

